✅2.2 What happens internally when start() is called with Runnable?

Example:
thread.start();

Internal flow:

Main Thread
      │ calls start()
      ▼
Thread.start()
      │
      ▼
JVM invokes native method
      │
      ▼
Operating System creates new Thread
      │
      ▼
Thread enters RUNNABLE state
      │
      ▼
Thread Scheduler selects it
      │
      ▼
Thread internally calls
task.run()

Important:

🔴 start() is used to start a new thread of execution.
🔴 When we call start(), it does not directly execute the Runnable's run() method.
🔴 It requests the JVM to create a new thread.
🔴 The JVM asks the Operating System to create the actual thread.
🔴 The thread enters the RUNNABLE state.
🔴 The Thread Scheduler decides when it gets CPU time.
🔴 The Thread object internally invokes the Runnable object's run() method.

Important Point

🔴 The Thread object is responsible for creating the execution path.
🔴 The Runnable object is responsible for providing the task.